---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const title = "Rethinking disability: the social model of disability";
---
<BaseLayout title={title}>
  <h1>{title}</h1>
  <p class="meta">My take on the paper.</p>

  <div class="paper">
    <p>
      The paper effectively critiques the medical model of disability and the need for a social model of disability. I agree with Sara Goering on the medical model of disability, having the tendency to “cure” PWD to make their body approximately like able bodied people.
    </p>

    <p>
      An example can be seen in CRISPR technology and the debate over whether it should be used to treat impairments that a person is born with or not. Deborah Kent, who is blind from birth, said she premised her life on the conviction that blindness was a neutral characteristic.
    </p>

    <p>
      The current concept of CRISPR technology is neither purely ethical nor purely unethical. And this is where the social model of disability is also important to consider, not just people who were born with impairments, but also scientists who are working on such powerful technology.
    </p>

    <p>
      The following chart shows a careful design with consideration of accessibility, and has components of the social model proposed by Sara Goering. However, if we take Deborah Kent’s example and if a fetus had an impairment such as hers, they would still be qualified for the CRISPR technology treatment, which doesn’t align with their need or want. In my opinion, the social model has the room to cover such cases as well.
    </p>

    <figure class="figure">
      <img src="/figures/reading-1/figure1.png" alt="Decision tree about disease vs disability framing" />
      <figcaption>Source figure : https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9007770/#Fig1</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      A critical element of the argument is distinguishing between healthy and unhealthy disabled individuals when designing assistive aids. The paper maintains that if an impairment does not negatively affect a person's life or future health, it may not require intervention, nor should these individuals necessarily be included in research on that impairment.
    </p>

    <p>
      By the definition, the healthy impairment in the bodily functions are predictable in the future, but the paper does not account for the mental health of the person, and in addition to their physical body. A person with a healthy disability might have predictable bodily functions but at the same time, the stigma of the disability could affect their mental health.
    </p>

    <p>
      Another addition that needs to be considered is the unhealthy but “healthy” appearing body. People with MDD, women on the neurodivergence spectrum appear to fit in the society but they are prone to burn out and decline in mental health.
    </p>

    <p style=”text-align: center;”>
      Citation:
    </p>

    <p>
      Goering, Sara. “Rethinking disability: the social model of disability and chronic disease.” Current Reviews in Musculoskeletal Medicine vol. 8, 2 (2015): 134–8. doi:10.1007/s12178-015-9273-z
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
